= The cost-effectiveness of recommended adjunctive osteoarthritis management options: Results from a computer simulation model
== OARSI World Congress, Toronto
#smallcaps[Date]\ May 2019

#smallcaps[Citation]\ #eval("Wilson R, Chua J, Briggs AM, Abbott JH. The cost-effectiveness of recommended adjunctive osteoarthritis management options: Results from a computer simulation model. _Osteoarthritis and Cartilage_ 2019;27(S1):S313. doi:~#link(\"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.joca.2019.02.711\")[10.1016/j.joca.2019.02.711]", mode: "markup")

=== Abstract
*Purpose*
To estimate the cost-effectiveness, from the perspective of the New Zealand
(NZ) healthcare system, of recommended adjunctive management options for
knee osteoarthritis (OA), delivered in addition to core long-term
management.

*Methods*
We have previously developed and validated a computer simulation model of
the natural history, treatment, health-related quality of life (HRQoL)
impacts, and healthcare costs of knee OA in the NZ population (the NZ-MOA
model). The NZ-MOA model is a state-transition, microsimulation model of
the population burden of knee OA in NZ. Within the model, individuals can
develop radiographic OA (defined by Kellgren-Lawrence grade), experience
HRQoL losses associated with OA, and undergo a sequence of treatments to
alleviate the HRQoL losses associated with OA or slow structural
progression of the disease. The model is run, for the same population
cohort, for a comparator treatment sequence and then a modified treatment
sequence including the new intervention(s) to be evaluated.
Cost-effectiveness of the new intervention is calculated from the
incremental lifetime QALYs gained and costs of the modified treatment
sequence compared with the comparator.

We used the NZ-MOA model to simulate the lifetime incremental QALY gains
and healthcare costs incurred of nine adjunctive management options
recommended by the 2018 Royal Australian College of General Practitioners
(RACGP) OA clinical practice guideline (CPG): aquatic-based exercise,
thermal therapy (heat pack), massage, assistive walking devices (walking
cane), cognitive behavioural therapy, oral non-steroidal anti-inflammatory
drugs (NSAIDs), topical NSAIDs, intra-articular corticosteroids, and
duloxetine.

The comparator treatment sequence consisted of “Core” management
interventions for knee OA (i.e. self-management and education and
land-based exercise, with weight management for overweight or obese
individuals), recommended by the RACGP CPG for all patients, and total
joint replacement (and possible revision surgery) for eligible patients
progressing to end-stage OA. The intervention treatment sequences, modelled
separately for each recommended intervention, added one of the nine
recommended adjunctive management options to Core treatment, and before
possible eligibility for joint replacement surgery.

Treatment effect estimates and adverse events were sourced from the RACGP
CPG and published systematic reviews. The primary HRQoL outcome was
improvement in SF-6D utility value. In a sensitivity analysis, we modelled
HRQoL based on the WOMAC pain subscale. Costs of treatment were calculated
using a bottom-up approach, by multiplying the resource use required to
provide each treatment by NZ reference prices for healthcare items and
services.

We calculated incremental costs and QALYs, incremental cost-effectiveness
ratios (ICERs), and net monetary benefits (INBs) at willingness-to-pay
(WTP) thresholds of one, two, and three times GDP per capita (\$52,300 in
base year 2013) for each of the interventions.

*Results*
All adjunctive interventions were effective in increasing population
lifetime QALYs relative to the Core treatment sequence (Table 1). Lifetime
QALY gains for the 2013 population cohort varied from 15 600 QALYs for
duloxetine to 54 600 QALYs for walking aids. Lifetime incremental costs
ranged from -\$105 per capita (cost saving) for walking cane, to \$4 899 per
capita for massage.

Walking cane and oral NSAIDs dominated core treatment only, with higher
lifetime QALYs and lower lifetime costs (due to reduced subsequent demand
for joint replacement surgery). At the 1xGDP per capita WTP level, INBs
were \$1 360 and \$473, respectively. Intra-articular corticosteroids were
also cost-effective at the 1xGDP per capita WTP level, with INBs of \$470
(Figure 1). Topical NSAIDs and cognitive behavioural therapy were
cost-effective at the 2xGDP per capita level, and aquatic-based exercise
became cost-effective at the 3xGDP level only. Duloxetine, thermal therapy,
or massage were not cost-effective at any considered WTP levels. Using the
WOMAC pain outcome did not change the overall cost-effectiveness findings
(except for aquatic-based exercise, which was no longer cost-effective at
3xGDP per capita).

*Conclusions*
Three of the nine recommended adjunctive interventions were found to be
highly cost-effective from the NZ healthcare system perspective, and a
further three were moderately cost-effective. In particular, low-cost,
early interventions that reduce demand for subsequent high-cost treatments
such as joint replacement surgery may realise substantial cost-savings over
the life-course of people with knee OA. These interventions should be
prioritised for further evaluation and consideration for more widespread
utilization in the NZ health system.

